WEBVTT

0
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.520
What does Africa mean to me...

1
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.560
Like Estonia,
a lovely part of the world.

2
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.640
Well, Africa to me means Ghana.

3
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:41.280
And kind of sweet...
I don't know how to answer it.

4
00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.960
Ghana - a former British Gold Coast,
is located in West Africa.

5
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.200
A country of which surface area
is equal to UK

6
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.640
could be a paradise on earth.

7
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.680
On the shores of the Atlantic Ocean
next to the Gulf of Guinea run sunny

8
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.480
sand beaches, grow coconut trees
and the water is always warm.

9
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:18.000
Ghana's bowels of the earth hide
so much gold, that it is being sold

10
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.440
in tens of tons every year
to the rest of the world.

11
00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.520
Ancient Shea trees grow in the
savannas of Northern Ghana

12
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.080
bearing valuable fruit,

13
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.560
and the people living there are
rather peaceful and hospitable.

14
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.880
Although the country is developing
and getting richer,

15
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.760
people living in the villages are
poor and make do with very little.

16
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:54.080
You cannot get cheese there. - Why?
- They don't have any cheese there.

17
00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:57.520
I should have warned you,

18
00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.000
so that you had taken your last
cheese sandwich with you.

19
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.360
When you are there for a long time
you will start craving for cheese.

20
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.080
The best thing is parmesan.

21
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:26.200
OUR WORLD
ESTONIAN VILLAGE IN GHANA

22
00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:34.920
Diana Tamm is a reformer. She studied
to be an art teacher and art critic,

23
00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.080
but ended up in Kongo village
in Ghana as a volunteer.

24
00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.280
Kongo village is located in the north
700 km from the capital city Accra.

25
00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.560
Being a volunteer became her job.

26
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.640
Now Diana helps to develop business
in Africa through NGO Mondo

27
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.720
in order to help better the quality
of life for the local women.

28
00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:57.320
Now I am visiting for the 5th time.

29
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.960
All my next visits to the Kongo
village will be dealing

30
00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.120
with basket weavers and production
and development of Shea butter.

31
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.800
Hi, hi! How are you?

32
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.880
The joy of reunion is great.

33
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.960
Village women waited for Diana's
arrival for months.

34
00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:35.720
Estonians who accompany her
are also welcomed as old friends.

35
00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:43.280
Welcome! Thank you!
Welcome! You say - Nambaa!

36
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.360
Kongo Village in North Ghana next to
the border of Burkina Faso

37
00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:05.160
is exactly one of those places

38
00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.680
where Ghana's economic development
reaches very slowly.

39
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.240
In the village of many
thousand people

40
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:14.280
women who have lost their husband
struggle the most

41
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.040
because the man is responsible for
providing for his family.

42
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:26.080
Diana's arrival is long awaited
because with her help tens of women

43
00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:28.840
in Kongo village are hopeful to start
their own business.

44
00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.480
With the help of Estonians

45
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.720
a local Shea butter factory will
hopefully be started.

46
00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:39.240
Shea butter is justifiably called
African women's gold,

47
00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.920
because both cosmetics
and food production industries

48
00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.240
have discovered this raw material and
there would be enough market for it.

49
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.280
Diana brings with her two business
women from Estonia

50
00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:53.000
who want to buy the Shea butter -
African women's gold.

51
00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.600
So you remember last time I was
telling you that there are

52
00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:00.240
some ladies who are interested in
your Shea butter,

53
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.880
but they wanted to come here but the
plains were not flying...

54
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.200
So we were waiting and waiting but
they could not come.

55
00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.040
I think we are very grateful
that they have come.

56
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.240
And you have a chance to talk to
each other and meet each other

57
00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:22.480
and discuss the business you could
do together. So I will introduce.

58
00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:32.480
This is Kati ... And she will have a
local name afterwards, right?

59
00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.840
And this is Kai.

60
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.520
A little Shea butter factory
is in a way

61
00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.000
like an Estonian Embassy
in the Kongo village.

62
00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.720
Women are proud to wave the Estonian
flag and both the gate and

63
00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.080
the workshop's wall are painted
blue, black and white.

64
00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:00.320
Today the elaborate reception
has been arranged

65
00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:02.200
for the guests who
have arrived from the mother country.

66
00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.040
Introducing Kai Lepik and
Kati Mäekalda is important

67
00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.440
because trust in Ghana's society

68
00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.840
is built through previous
acquaintances and friendship.

69
00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:15.120
Every guest receives a new name that
bears a certain local meaning

70
00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:19.000
referring to their personal identity
or to some god.

71
00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:24.440
Arrival of the guests and reception
with the dance gives both parties

72
00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:27.000
hope that the business will
be fruitful.

73
00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.640
Estonians don't want to
be worse dancers than Ghana women

74
00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:35.800
and make their feet skip fast.
The trust is built that way.

75
00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.800
A Shea butter factory
has been built here by Mondo.

76
00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.600
We would want to start exporting it
to EU and America

77
00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:36.000
that is why we are
here. We will try to do it.

78
00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:44.440
As a surprise to us, Shea butter is
being used mainly in food production.

79
00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.120
We knew that it was being used
more in cosmetics industry.

80
00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:52.920
We will start selling the Shea butter
first to private consumers

81
00:07:53.240 --> 00:08:00.320
and start searching for people and
bigger organisations

82
00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.160
that will use it in cosmetics
industry.

83
00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.720
Shea tree grows naturally in dry
savannas

84
00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:18.880
in central zone of African
continent.

85
00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:22.600
The fruit of the tree is sour-sweet
and very nutritious.

86
00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:25.840
In the middle of the pulp there is a
part of the nut that is rich in oil

87
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:27.880
and today has become like gold.

88
00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:32.360
The tree starts bearing fruit only in
10 - 15 years

89
00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:35.360
and does that for two centuries.

90
00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:39.880
Shea butter is amazing in many ways -
in cosmetics industry

91
00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.600
Shea butter is valued for its
moisture and healing properties.

92
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:46.360
Unrefined butter is especially
valuable because producing it

93
00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.840
is a time consuming handicraft.
Locals use Shea butter mainly

94
00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:55.200
for cooking purposes -
a spoonful of Shea butter

95
00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:59.560
gives the food a good nutty taste,
adding nutrients and vitamins.

96
00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.640
We bring the nuts from office.

97
00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.160
HOSTESS OF THE SHEA CO-OPERATIVE

98
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:13.000
We wash them. After washing
we dry it. After drying we take it

99
00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.040
to the crushing we do the crushing,
after the crushing,

100
00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:27.960
we bring it back to our kitchen.
We roast it up to one hour. Or more.

101
00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:31.240
It depends how you feed to fire.

102
00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:37.840
During the picking season
they are normally wet.

103
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:43.600
But now they are dry and you can
roast them for one hour.

104
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:47.880
After one hour you cool it down.
And you take it to the grand mill.

105
00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:53.720
You mill it. Bring it out again.
Add water.

106
00:09:53.840 --> 00:10:00.160
You do the kneading,
mix it and extract oil.

107
00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:08.200
Then you take it back to the fire.
You boil for some time.

108
00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:12.360
You get the oil
and leave the rest there.

109
00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:27.000
Shea butter production starts in the
factory's courtyard after the fruit

110
00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.800
has been picked
and the nuts are in bags.

111
00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:31.680
Women wash the nuts,

112
00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:37.040
place them to dry in the sun and pick
out the unsuitable nuts one by one.

113
00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:43.360
Sorted dry nuts need to be crushed -
even that was hand craft before.

114
00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:49.240
While crushing the mill room is
filled with sweet cocoa like smell,

115
00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:51.360
even though the nuts themselves
taste bitter.

116
00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:01.160
Crushed nuts will be roasted
in a closed drum on an open fire.

117
00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:03.280
It all takes time

118
00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:07.800
and adds more heat to a day
that is already 40 degree heat.

119
00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:18.600
Roasted nuts will be dried again
in the sun.

120
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.240
Crushed and roasted nuts
will be ground fine in the mill

121
00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:26.080
and will become a creamy melted
chocolate like mixture.

122
00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:35.480
Brown paste will need to be mixed
little by little with water

123
00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:41.440
followed by strong kneading.
Bending over the bowl work

124
00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:45.040
and working with hand in the desert
heat will slowly bring the fat

125
00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:49.640
from Shea nut to the surface.
The separated mass is Shea butter

126
00:11:49.880 --> 00:11:52.440
that needs to be drained
with water many times.

127
00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:56.600
Shea butter is washed manually
many times.

128
00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:00.600
At last the Shea butter
needs to be boiled.

129
00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:04.760
Work will go on the whole day and
boiling starts only in the dark.

130
00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:10.400
Golden yellow boiling hot butter
is lifted to a clean container

131
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:13.560
and left to cool until the morning.

132
00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:17.640
Opportunity to produce Shea butter
from beginning to an end

133
00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.720
in the new factory is a big step
forward for Kongo village women

134
00:12:20.840 --> 00:12:23.520
and more important
than we can imagine.

135
00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:31.480
It is important, because it has
opened our eyes and our ability,

136
00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:34.840
to come together, to help each other.

137
00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:38.680
It has now brought unity
to us widows who are here.

138
00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:45.800
We can now come together, work and
after we can all have some income

139
00:12:45.920 --> 00:12:47.760
to bring home to our families.

140
00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.840
Shea butter has cooled down by the
morning and the packaging begins.

141
00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.120
Those women have passed the health
inspection earlier,

142
00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:05.480
washed their hands thoroughly and are
wearing work uniform. The Shea butter

143
00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.760
cannot be contaminated with
impurities or bacteria

144
00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:10.600
after being heated up.

145
00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:18.400
Standardising the packaging creates
problems at first,

146
00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:20.680
because weighing
is done here for the first time.

147
00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.040
Market here measures
things by piece or by volume.

148
00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:29.400
Now the women need to start looking
at the weight and measure it,

149
00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:31.720
how much is that 100 or 200 grams

150
00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:34.600
that gets left over
from the 5 kg packet.

151
00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.400
Bystander might get an impression

152
00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:38.280
that the packer doesn't know the
numbers.

153
00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:44.800
An order for fifty kilos of
Shea butter

154
00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:47.000
to African women was given
by Estonians.

155
00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:49.600
A long and tedious process
needs to be undergone

156
00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:51.280
in order for the product
to be exported officially.

157
00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:54.880
Unfortunately the new factory does
not have a certificate at the moment

158
00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:58.400
and therefore the product travels to
Europe in small quantities

159
00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:00.080
in the clients' suitcases.

160
00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:03.920
By the way, the Shea butter was
almost confiscated

161
00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:06.040
later at the airport
being considered an explosive.

162
00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:11.240
This feeling that you get here,

163
00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:16.160
you feel how spoiled we are
coming from the West.

164
00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:18.360
Estonians are a small nation.

165
00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.480
Even though, we consider
ourselves nature centred, etc.

166
00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:24.920
They live here with very little and
are very happy!

167
00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:33.840
Their joy and welcome was so sincere
and real when we arrived. Heartful.

168
00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:44.640
They are so thankful first of all to
Mondo who came to this village.

169
00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:48.160
Because Mondo chooses these areas
that are struggling.

170
00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:52.120
These women have no work they have
nothing to do because

171
00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:54.320
this is like desert here.

172
00:14:54.440 --> 00:15:00.160
This is their only chance.
This project is their only

173
00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:02.720
opportunity because they themselves
cannot find any clients.

174
00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:08.240
Being packaged for Europeans,

175
00:15:08.360 --> 00:15:11.240
after a long flight Shea butter can
be bought in Tallinn.

176
00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:16.720
But producing Shea butter
is not the only thing

177
00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:19.080
that Mondo is helping with in Ghana.

178
00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.160
Baskets...

179
00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:39.040
The baskets get squashed when we
transport them because

180
00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:42.040
we press them into the bags so that
we can fit more of them in.

181
00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:06.480
Every basket
gets a label of the weaver.

182
00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:08.360
And when these baskets arrive

183
00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:13.800
we have asked that they attach the
names of the weavers to the baskets.

184
00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:16.800
And we also
take photos of the weavers.

185
00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:21.440
We print them in Estonia. According
to the name label on the basket,

186
00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:23.800
it gets the weaver's photo as well.

187
00:16:24.280 --> 00:16:31.760
I can see how important it is to
the buyers in Estonia, they ask -

188
00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:36.880
really, did that woman weave it?
And yes, that woman wove it.

189
00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:41.880
Good morning, how are you?
- I am fine, How are you?

190
00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.160
Weaving the baskets is an
income source for women in Ghana

191
00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:50.760
who get paid very little for this
work, sometimes even so little

192
00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:55.640
that a ride on the bus to the market
eats up all they make.

193
00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:59.560
Twenty five cents for the basket is
not much.

194
00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:03.120
At the same time weaving baskets to
be sold in Estonia

195
00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:06.960
and getting the right price makes
them make more effort for quality.

196
00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:09.160
Those were our basket weavers.

197
00:17:13.120 --> 00:17:17.760
At the beginning we had a small group
of basket weavers - about 8 women.

198
00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:21.080
Three of them are actually
from the main group.

199
00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.640
They are more experienced weavers.

200
00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:26.560
They used to weave
by themselves before

201
00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:31.040
but now that we have a co-operative,
they weave together.

202
00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:34.720
The group divides the orders
that have been placed from Estonia

203
00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:37.680
and help each other
with the quality testing

204
00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:40.760
making sure
that the patterns are right, etc.

205
00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:44.320
Two of them call each other sisters

206
00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:47.600
when in reality
their husbands are brothers.

207
00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:51.080
They live close
to each other's households.

208
00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:56.480
We shouldn't forget that Diana
has studied to be an art critic

209
00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.520
and she has very cleverly combined
her knowledge,

210
00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.440
the hand craft skills of Ghana women

211
00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.000
and the wishes and paying ability of
Estonian women.

212
00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:11.640
My work is mainly quality testing
and giving the women indications

213
00:18:11.760 --> 00:18:16.440
as to which quality the Western buyer
is actually expecting.

214
00:18:16.560 --> 00:18:20.320
All those baskets that they are
weaving here

215
00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:25.280
will be sold mainly in Estonia but
there are also clients

216
00:18:25.400 --> 00:18:26.400
from America and other places.

217
00:18:27.320 --> 00:18:32.920
The other thing that we have done
is that all those patterns and models

218
00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:36.440
have actually been created by
Estonian designers.

219
00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:42.320
I also make sure that the patterns
turn out the way we have agreed

220
00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:48.160
and the shape and size is right.
It is work you do with your hands

221
00:18:48.280 --> 00:18:50.680
and they are not used to
measuring things here.

222
00:18:50.800 --> 00:18:53.520
I am the one who measures things

223
00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:56.720
and say when the basket is too wide

224
00:18:56.840 --> 00:18:59.800
or something should be done
differently.

225
00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:05.240
We have this kind of catalogue here
that we use for placing the orders

226
00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:07.440
and they can keep an eye
on the orders.

227
00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.200
There is also a photo of
what the basket model

228
00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:14.000
looks like that they use
as an example for weaving.

229
00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:16.160
If it is important to
the Estonian buyers

230
00:19:16.280 --> 00:19:18.400
to know who has weaved their basket

231
00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:21.320
then it is also important to
the women in Kongo village

232
00:19:21.440 --> 00:19:24.040
who will buy their basket.

233
00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:26.360
Just lately Kodukant,
the Estonian Village Movement,

234
00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:28.000
ordered baskets from Kongo village

235
00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:31.000
weavers for the recognition cause
for excellent Estonian volunteers.

236
00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:34.560
The baskets were handed over
by president Toomas Hendrik Ilves

237
00:19:34.960 --> 00:19:37.760
and naturally the women were proud
because it was their first time

238
00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:42.160
to weave baskets
for such an important client.

239
00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:44.800
So this is the president of Estonia.

240
00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:49.360
And the ladies here were weaving
baskets for him

241
00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:54.000
so he can present them
to the volunteers.

242
00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:58.440
Unlike Shea butter
the baskets weigh little

243
00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:00.920
and it is important to send them to
Estonia by post also.

244
00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:05.480
In addition exporting baskets
doesn't need any special permission.

245
00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:08.360
Thanks to regular orders

246
00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:11.800
it is possible for Kongo village
women to count on that income.

247
00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:16.960
In addition with help from Estonians
their little workshop has been fixed

248
00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:20.200
and a secure fence
has been built around it.

249
00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:24.760
We get asked a lot
why we are working in Africa

250
00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:30.080
and not in Estonia like in Valga or
Põlva County or in Eastern Estonia.

251
00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:35.080
But we say that there are plenty of
organisations who are located on

252
00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:39.280
the outskirts of Estonia. We exist so
that Estonian people

253
00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:43.880
can work in Africa through an
Estonian organisation.

254
00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:49.200
I always think about this question
from a human point of view,

255
00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:56.640
what is the difference between
Estonian children being hungry

256
00:20:56.760 --> 00:21:00.200
or children in Ghana being hungry?

257
00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:03.160
Every person in the world
should be fed.

258
00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.840
Estonians' activity is
the only source

259
00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:15.880
for economic growth for this village.

260
00:21:16.840 --> 00:21:19.040
It is true that money surplus is also
provided by those

261
00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:20.760
who get their pay from the government

262
00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:25.720
teachers, county officials -
or illegal goldminers.

263
00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.320
Otherwise the money is being circled
within the village

264
00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:32.600
from sewer to the innkeeper,
from innkeeper to weaver,

265
00:21:32.720 --> 00:21:34.640
from weaver to the stall keeper.

266
00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:42.040
Market is the heart of this village.
The market day is every third day.

267
00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:43.720
It has been divided in a way

268
00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:46.280
that one day
one village has a market day,

269
00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:49.760
on another day there is a market day
in the city

270
00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:54.280
and on the third day there
is a market day in a bigger city.

271
00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:56.920
The market is organised in rotation.

272
00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:00.280
Every time you come to the village
it is important to ask when the next

273
00:22:00.400 --> 00:22:05.960
market day will be, that is when you
can buy your tomatoes and bananas.

274
00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:10.960
There are only a few stalls open
on a daily basis

275
00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:13.960
selling biscuits, soap and water.

276
00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:17.840
But most of the trading takes place
on a market day.

277
00:22:19.400 --> 00:22:24.960
Market day is also for people coming
together and socialising.

278
00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:32.360
It is the heart of village life.

279
00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:41.280
A lot of women trade in small ways
in addition to their farming.

280
00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:47.240
For example they buy 3 kg of soap
and then sell it piece by piece

281
00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:52.240
and earn a little profit that way,
which of course is very small.

282
00:22:52.840 --> 00:23:00.440
It gives them a reason to be active
and meet other women.

283
00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:03.160
Good morning!

284
00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:07.120
Let's look at the market.

285
00:23:07.240 --> 00:23:11.840
The main trading areas are underneath
the little shelters.

286
00:23:12.880 --> 00:23:16.120
They sell garden produce
that they have grown.

287
00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:36.400
There are no scales here.
They sell by volume,

288
00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:46.120
either putting something in the bowl
and pricing the bowl

289
00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:52.120
or they build these towers and the
tower has a price.

290
00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:57.560
In general you
cannot bargain much.

291
00:23:57.840 --> 00:24:00.320
Things have their fixed
market prices.

292
00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.560
Sometimes they add things to you.

293
00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:06.600
I was given a bowlful of tomatoes
and then she gave me

294
00:24:06.760 --> 00:24:12.000
three more tomatoes,
that was the bonus I was given.

295
00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:15.160
Mpoo'ya (thank you)

296
00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:22.200
This bag of tomatoes costs 25 cents.
One local currency is Ghana cedi.

297
00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:33.840
Mango, how much? 50 pesawas.
It is mango season right now.

298
00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:38.800
Mangos are so yummy, but I can see
that these ones are quite soft.

299
00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:41.280
I take this one.

300
00:24:44.280 --> 00:24:50.400
What are you buying?
- Mango. - They said they want beans.

301
00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:56.040
Ahaa, you are buying for Lisa.

302
00:24:56.160 --> 00:24:59.880
You are buying for them or they will
come and buy?

303
00:25:00.480 --> 00:25:03.960
I am buying for them,
but the money is with them.

304
00:25:06.040 --> 00:25:11.520
This lady is Madam Rose Adazebra.

305
00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:16.600
She is a like a local activist here.

306
00:25:17.400 --> 00:25:21.880
She is a member
of our basket weavers group

307
00:25:22.000 --> 00:25:25.600
and also a treasurer of our
partner organisation KoCDA.

308
00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:27.440
She takes care
of their money matters.

309
00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:33.120
It is interesting that not many women
from her generation are educated.

310
00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:37.560
Good morning! How are you?
- I'm fine, how are you, too?

311
00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:49.360
Rose is an interesting woman in that
sense that not many women in

312
00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:52.120
her generation are educated, not many
had an opportunity to attend school.

313
00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.400
She is between 50 to 60 years old.

314
00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:59.320
She decided to go and educate herself

315
00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:02.280
and is one of the few women from her
generation who is literate.

316
00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:06.760
Therefore she has taken on the role
to look after the money.

317
00:26:07.720 --> 00:26:09.800
Rose is also very active
in the village.

318
00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:15.400
She organises training for women from
her region and also looks after women

319
00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:19.240
who have given birth, training them
in breast feeding and so on.

320
00:26:20.320 --> 00:26:26.600
It is good to see here what is being
bought and sold in the village.

321
00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:30.920
They mainly grow different grains.

322
00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:35.680
It keeps quite well in this climate

323
00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:41.080
and when they want to cook something
from it, they grind a needed portion.

324
00:26:41.560 --> 00:26:47.720
If I understand correctly, these are
variations of some sort of corn.

325
00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:55.880
This should be a Guinea corn.

326
00:26:56.000 --> 00:26:59.880
I don't know how that is in Estonian.
Some sort of corn variation.

327
00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:06.040
This is also corn? - Soy.
- Ah, soybeans?

328
00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:08.600
And this one is millet?
- Yes.

329
00:27:10.600 --> 00:27:12.760
And this one too millet?

330
00:27:16.080 --> 00:27:19.240
Morning. - Morning.
- What is it?

331
00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:25.240
Its for putting farm animals inside.
- Like chicken?

332
00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:30.960
As I understand this is
like a chicken cage.

333
00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:33.680
If you need to transport your
chicken, then you put it in there.

334
00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:40.360
This man here is a shoe repairer. He
is repairing galebash right now.

335
00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:44.360
Galebash is a pumpkin,
which they dry.

336
00:27:44.680 --> 00:27:48.880
They grow these pumpkins especially
for making different dishes.

337
00:27:51.440 --> 00:27:53.720
It is being used an
ordinary bowl then.

338
00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:58.240
But it is especially popular for
drinking a local home made beer.

339
00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:06.640
How are things going? Good!
Tidii. Thank you!

340
00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:13.440
What he said to me tidii right now
means that you have been invited

341
00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:17.120
to take part of the food. Whenever
someone is eating,

342
00:28:17.240 --> 00:28:20.600
then in English you say
"you are invited".

343
00:28:21.480 --> 00:28:31.080
What are you eating? What is it?
TZ? No-no, it's banku.

344
00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:35.360
She was one of the women
who produce Shea butter.

345
00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:43.120
TZ ( Tii Zeed ) or Tuo Zaafi

346
00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:45.520
is one of the most widespread foods
in Northern Ghana.

347
00:28:45.640 --> 00:28:49.200
It is a porridge made from corn flour
and potato like manioc flour that

348
00:28:49.600 --> 00:28:52.560
is mostly being eaten with soup
made from green vegetables.

349
00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:57.360
Basket weaver Rose invited Diana
for dinner tonight and naturally will

350
00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.600
serve her the same national dish.

351
00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:06.600
They are making us the same local
dish that is called Tii Zeed.

352
00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:12.760
Actually it is
the main food here.

353
00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:17.600
Some families have it for breakfast
and for dinner every day.

354
00:29:18.120 --> 00:29:25.440
It turns into a very thick porridge
and is eaten with a sauce.

355
00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:28.440
The sauce has already been made
in the other pot.

356
00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:37.200
They say here - a good woman
is able to make a good Tii Zeed .

357
00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:45.400
Afterwards the porridge will be
beaten in a way that requires skill.

358
00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:58.080
They put in two kinds of millet
flour and water. That's it.

359
00:30:52.080 --> 00:30:53.840
Because this is the custom in
North Ghana,

360
00:30:53.960 --> 00:30:55.480
Rose eats the hot porridge
with hands.

361
00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:59.720
It is part of respecting traditions
and furthermore,

362
00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:01.560
the food is tastier that way.

363
00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:06.520
For the guest there is boiled
goat's meat

364
00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:08.840
that is not on the table every day.

365
00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:18.480
Rose's husband died
in the car accident years ago.

366
00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:21.520
Her husband is buried right next
to the house

367
00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:24.960
and Rose placed a big stone pile
on the grave

368
00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:27.160
where she can sit and have rest
close to her husband.

369
00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:33.600
Rose's income is small for a widow
and opportunity to weave baskets

370
00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:38.120
is important for her,
so that she can eat Tuo Zaafi.

371
00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:46.480
A lot of men who have money troubles
are involved in illegal gold mining.

372
00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:56.200
They have this little gold miners
centre here.

373
00:31:56.320 --> 00:32:03.120
There are lots of little gold mines
in this area.

374
00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:08.080
On the surface mines where
they just dig a hole.

375
00:32:08.200 --> 00:32:13.040
There is a network of buyers,
small buyers,

376
00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:17.040
who buy from those who sieve
gold in the mines.

377
00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:21.280
There are those who sell it on
to the next person,

378
00:32:21.400 --> 00:32:23.200
who then sells it to someone for
export.

379
00:32:23.320 --> 00:32:27.000
Here in a little village they use
mercury

380
00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:33.920
underneath the shelter to
melt the gold.

381
00:32:36.600 --> 00:32:39.400
Kwame is an attentive and helpful
young man.

382
00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:42.400
He is one of the many young people

383
00:32:42.520 --> 00:32:44.600
who hopes to establish himself with
gold mining.

384
00:32:45.480 --> 00:32:47.560
A man has to have money
to marry a woman.

385
00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:53.720
Kwame knows exactly how to get
the precious metal from the gold ore.

386
00:32:54.640 --> 00:33:01.120
We go to the bush, to some place
where we go and find.

387
00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:03.560
We dig it down and find the stones.

388
00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:06.960
We know which stones have gold and
which ones don't have.

389
00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:12.440
We bring it here
to the crushing place.

390
00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:17.240
Then we take it
to the grinding machine place.

391
00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:21.600
When you finish there,
then you bring it here to wash it.

392
00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:29.320
Then we take the mercury and
put it in to remove the gold.

393
00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:33.240
That is how we do it.

394
00:33:33.520 --> 00:33:38.440
Local men mine gold in many
surrounding unregistered mines.

395
00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:42.040
Even though officially it
is forbidden,

396
00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:44.400
the local authority
makes exceptions

397
00:33:44.520 --> 00:33:49.360
because gold mining brings in money
for many families.

398
00:33:49.480 --> 00:33:52.680
Men in return are
not bothered by the restrictions

399
00:33:52.880 --> 00:33:57.280
and look quite openly for ore from
old deserted mines as well

400
00:33:57.400 --> 00:34:00.160
as from new underground passages
that they have dug themselves.

401
00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:05.440
This is our father's land.

402
00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:11.560
Nobody can come
and disturb us not to work.

403
00:34:11.680 --> 00:34:15.000
This is our fore-forefather's land.

404
00:34:17.080 --> 00:34:19.760
These rough men
are actually quite friendly

405
00:34:19.880 --> 00:34:25.040
and allow the film crew to climb into
the gold mine.

406
00:34:25.160 --> 00:34:27.440
Can you follow me?

407
00:34:29.080 --> 00:34:34.120
The passages of this seemingly small
mine reach as long as 100 meters.

408
00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:38.280
There are no stairs to get down
there. In order to descend

409
00:34:38.400 --> 00:34:40.720
you need to be like a Spiderman with
your legs and hands spread out

410
00:34:40.840 --> 00:34:43.320
leaning against the walls
of the passage.

411
00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:47.120
Wooden frames are only
at the beginning of the entrance.

412
00:34:51.760 --> 00:34:53.800
Am I good?
- Yes, come

413
00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:57.680
In 16 meters deep the cameraman
is already out of breath.

414
00:34:58.120 --> 00:35:03.200
Here you are working?
- No-no.

415
00:35:03.320 --> 00:35:06.800
Not really this place.

416
00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:29.000
Don't go too far!

417
00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:36.320
Come, we want you to see clear!

418
00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:49.320
Still going? - Yes!
- Can I get up afterwards?

419
00:35:49.760 --> 00:35:52.040
Don't worry about that!

420
00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:00.680
Rising back up alive
is more important

421
00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:04.040
than filming rare frames
and he climbs back up.

422
00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:08.240
This gold mine
does not have ventilation.

423
00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:11.600
During the last few years three men

424
00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:14.640
have lost their lives here,
they descended too early

425
00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:17.600
after the demolition and did not
have enough oxygen in the passages.

426
00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:24.040
What is there?
- Dark, there is no air.

427
00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:27.440
Although gold mining in
unofficial mines is very dangerous,

428
00:36:27.560 --> 00:36:31.000
locals say that
they do it day and night.

429
00:36:31.880 --> 00:36:34.640
Men rest from tiredness by sleeping
on the stones,

430
00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:36.760
women also go to the mines

431
00:36:36.880 --> 00:36:39.960
but only to bring food and to make
sure that the men are fed.

432
00:36:58.440 --> 00:37:00.440
Initial treatment of gold
takes place publicly

433
00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:02.640
by the limestone square
in village centre,

434
00:37:03.040 --> 00:37:05.280
where others are playing football.

435
00:37:05.400 --> 00:37:08.360
Nothing is hidden
in this illegal activity.

436
00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:12.560
Ore is brought to the mill in bags
being carried on the motorcycle.

437
00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:16.520
The rock will be crushed
and then ground.

438
00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:20.320
Remember African women's gold -

439
00:37:20.480 --> 00:37:24.960
Shea tree nut had to be crushed and
ground.

440
00:37:25.080 --> 00:37:27.520
Same will be done with men's
gold,

441
00:37:27.920 --> 00:37:30.520
the machinery even seems to be
the same.

442
00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:34.640
Ground rocks are brought outside
from a dark mill again

443
00:37:34.760 --> 00:37:36.360
and washing with water next.

444
00:37:37.360 --> 00:37:40.600
If Shea butter rises to top when
mixed with water

445
00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:43.040
then gold sinks to the bottom -

446
00:37:43.800 --> 00:37:47.880
using that gold property a cloth is
placed on the bottom of the gutter.

447
00:37:48.480 --> 00:37:51.840
When rinsing the crushed and ground
gold ore with water

448
00:37:51.960 --> 00:37:55.120
gold sticks on the cloth
and sand runs off it.

449
00:37:56.360 --> 00:37:59.160
Then the gold dust will be washed
from the cloth

450
00:37:59.280 --> 00:38:02.280
which is still mixed with mud.

451
00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:06.040
A dangerous method is used

452
00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:09.920
in order to bring the gold together
into a nugget.

453
00:38:17.000 --> 00:38:22.360
Kwame uses mercury to get the gold.
We remember from chemistry lesson

454
00:38:22.600 --> 00:38:25.920
that gold mixed with mercury forms
an amalgam - alloy.

455
00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:29.600
This is the way to bring gold dust
from the mixture of minerals

456
00:38:29.720 --> 00:38:31.960
together with mercury into one
nugget.

457
00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:35.760
The youngsters have heard that
mercury is extremely poisonous

458
00:38:35.880 --> 00:38:39.880
but they are not aware of real risks
involved with doing this work.

459
00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:43.840
Liquid metal is being added with
bare hands until the fingers hold

460
00:38:43.960 --> 00:38:47.040
the amalgam of mercury and gold.

461
00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:50.040
After this
the most dangerous part begins -

462
00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:54.080
heating the formed ball
with blowtorch.

463
00:38:54.200 --> 00:38:58.400
The aim of that is to vaporise the
mercuryin order to have pure gold.

464
00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:00.520
At the same time we know

465
00:39:00.640 --> 00:39:02.440
that the vapours of mercury are most
dangerous.

466
00:39:02.680 --> 00:39:06.160
Even breathing in
big amounts of mercury vapours

467
00:39:06.760 --> 00:39:09.160
for a short time
can cause damage to lungs.

468
00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:12.560
The result of the damage in
more serious cases

469
00:39:12.680 --> 00:39:16.600
is permanent brain and kidney damage
or even death.

470
00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:20.840
Those young men are not even using

471
00:39:20.960 --> 00:39:23.840
a gas mask and risk their lives
for income.

472
00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:30.960
In addition to the release of toxic
fumes and breathing them in,

473
00:39:31.600 --> 00:39:37.720
the soil becomes polluted and
mercury gets into the food chain.

474
00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:47.400
Local buyer is right there,
the mill belongs to him.

475
00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:50.440
100 Euros will be divided between
over twenty men

476
00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:54.960
that will have to pay the diggers and
also the ones treating the gold,

477
00:39:55.080 --> 00:39:57.360
of whom all have put their lives
on the line.

478
00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:01.440
That way each man will make
about 5 to 10 Euros.

479
00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:04.520
Considering the selling price of gold
it is incredibly cheap

480
00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:07.120
but many times more compared

481
00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:09.160
to what the women make
from basket weaving.

482
00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:15.160
But yes, gold mining is a lucky dip.

483
00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:20.280
It is lucky and unlucky and income at
the same time.

484
00:40:20.520 --> 00:40:25.080
You can try your luck
and dig for gold

485
00:40:25.200 --> 00:40:28.480
but not go and study instead and
learn a profession.

486
00:40:28.840 --> 00:40:33.960
You hope that you are lucky one day
and find a big piece of gold.

487
00:40:34.640 --> 00:40:38.840
But the income is very unstable and
you cannot be sure

488
00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:44.360
whether you will find anything during
six months or you might find very

489
00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:48.280
small quantities. At the same time
you might hear of others who found

490
00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:51.840
a big piece of gold
and ride a motorcycle in the village.

491
00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:56.680
So there are different
social problems that go with it.

492
00:40:56.800 --> 00:41:01.320
Kwame is on of the young men
who has not finished his education.

493
00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:05.440
He needed his friend's help in order
to write his full name.

494
00:41:12.120 --> 00:41:15.920
Money coming from gold is important
for all the other lines of activity

495
00:41:16.200 --> 00:41:19.840
and that is why the authorities
do not bother the men.

496
00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:23.320
Money that has been earned from
gold buys plenty of other services

497
00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:24.680
offered by Kongo village,

498
00:41:25.000 --> 00:41:28.200
for example there are
over 10 sewing workshops ...

499
00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:32.120
In spite of being poor
people dress quite well.

500
00:41:32.920 --> 00:41:35.640
Men have their own tailors and
women have their own.

501
00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:44.280
Money also goes to hairdressers
because the hair needs to look good:

502
00:41:44.520 --> 00:41:48.080
again women have women hairdressers
and men are being serviced by men.

503
00:41:52.280 --> 00:41:55.760
And money also goes on transportation
the village even has one taxi.

504
00:41:56.160 --> 00:42:00.080
Cars are quite old.
They are written off cars

505
00:42:00.200 --> 00:42:05.680
that have probably been brought
somewhere from Europe or West.

506
00:42:06.560 --> 00:42:09.440
Although they might look quite good
at the beginning

507
00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:17.080
they will have dents in them soon
because of the traffic culture.

508
00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:26.600
If the politicians need to go some-
where finer cars speed around here.

509
00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:31.920
Sometimes small buses speed
through the holes on the road,

510
00:42:32.880 --> 00:42:37.560
but the speed on these bumpy roads
should not be more than 20 km/h.

511
00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:41.000
In reality these bumps on the roads
are quite dangerous, especially when

512
00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:45.240
riding a motorbike. Everybody tries
to ride around the holes on the road,

513
00:42:45.360 --> 00:42:49.720
and the right or left lane
get muddled.

514
00:42:49.840 --> 00:42:55.240
If you are not careful there might be
a head on collision.

515
00:42:55.600 --> 00:42:57.560
Time to time you can hear news of

516
00:42:57.680 --> 00:43:02.680
someone getting killed in traffic
accident.

517
00:43:02.800 --> 00:43:07.800
When I was here about a year ago
there was a school director's husband

518
00:43:07.920 --> 00:43:11.640
who got killed that way.
He was on his bike

519
00:43:11.800 --> 00:43:20.080
and a big truck basically ran over
him. Tough.

520
00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:25.880
We are little bit in a hurry
so make it fast.

521
00:43:26.720 --> 00:43:29.400
What is he doing there?
- I don't know what he is doing.

522
00:43:55.120 --> 00:43:58.440
Little stop over to get petrol.

523
00:43:58.560 --> 00:44:04.800
We got 5 l of petrol this is how they
buy it here - in small quantities.

524
00:44:04.920 --> 00:44:08.320
No one
has enough money here to buy a lot.

525
00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:10.840
They buy 2 spoons of sugar.

526
00:44:10.960 --> 00:44:15.080
They buy a litre for the bike and for
the taxi they buy 5 litres of petrol.

527
00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:25.360
Naturally the pub keeper gets a share
from the gold money,

528
00:44:25.480 --> 00:44:27.400
whereas the easiest way to start a
business

529
00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:29.560
in the village is to open a pub.

530
00:44:30.040 --> 00:44:31.920
So the way it is here with pubs,

531
00:44:32.120 --> 00:44:36.800
I am not sure what it makes then -
20 pubs per 7000 people or even more.

532
00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:39.960
Quite many.

533
00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:44.880
Let's peek into the pub as well.

534
00:44:47.360 --> 00:44:52.600
Good morning. How are you? Fine?

535
00:44:55.720 --> 00:45:02.640
An important part of the pub is a TV
in the corner. Bar counter has been

536
00:45:02.800 --> 00:45:08.000
covered with net just in case that
no one will reach over the counter.

537
00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:15.960
The main drinks here are beer and
soft drinks.

538
00:45:16.840 --> 00:45:24.320
One of the local soft drinks here is
Malta produced by Guiness.

539
00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:27.800
Local beers are Star and Club.

540
00:45:27.920 --> 00:45:30.680
There are stronger drinks as well.

541
00:45:39.920 --> 00:45:44.360
Beer costs 1 Euro,
people here seem responsible

542
00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:47.560
but alcoholism is becoming a
serious problem.

543
00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:51.480
That man goes from pub to pub and
entertains people.

544
00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:41.560
That bar building is an example of
contemporary architecture.

545
00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:47.560
Traditional houses are made from
clay and have grass roof.

546
00:46:48.520 --> 00:46:51.920
Everyone has a dream to build
a house that is sturdier.

547
00:46:52.320 --> 00:46:57.840
There are these cement blocks that
are cast and then plastered.

548
00:46:58.040 --> 00:47:01.960
The house then gets a
metal or tin roof.

549
00:47:02.080 --> 00:47:06.400
In a way it is unpractical because

550
00:47:06.600 --> 00:47:11.520
the metal becomes very hot in the sun
and makes the rooms very hot.

551
00:47:11.800 --> 00:47:16.000
At the same time it lasts longer. If
termites eat through your grass roof

552
00:47:16.120 --> 00:47:18.360
in two years then metal roof will
last much longer.

553
00:47:18.880 --> 00:47:23.320
And in order to bring more air into
the building

554
00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:29.400
the cement blocks have holes in them
that allow the air to move.

555
00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:33.000
Glass windows are not being used much

556
00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:36.000
because they don't allow
the air to move much.

557
00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:44.920
The schools don't have windows
but simple window and door shutters.

558
00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:47.960
They are left open during the day.

559
00:47:50.200 --> 00:47:53.880
Southern coast soothes the desert
heat of North Ghana.

560
00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:57.880
Locals seldom traveldown to the
ocean because the trip is expensive

561
00:47:58.000 --> 00:48:00.040
and takes a whole day or night.

562
00:48:01.160 --> 00:48:04.240
Diana has a reason to go to the
capital city Accra

563
00:48:04.360 --> 00:48:06.360
and if she goes to the sea

564
00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:09.200
then going to the beach is definitely
a thing to do.

565
00:48:09.320 --> 00:48:10.880
South has a paradise climate -

566
00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:14.200
there is plenty of wind and sun,
water is warm.

567
00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:17.400
Even though Ghana is one country,
other nations live on the coast

568
00:48:17.520 --> 00:48:19.720
and houses are also built
differently.

569
00:48:22.320 --> 00:48:30.800
You can see proper architecture here.
These here are holiday houses.

570
00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:34.240
But in reality the houses in the
village are built the same way.

571
00:48:35.160 --> 00:48:38.840
If houses are built from clay in
North Ghana

572
00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:42.240
then they don't have clay here,
only a sand surface.

573
00:48:43.000 --> 00:48:49.280
The easiest way to build a house is
to weave together palm tree leaves.

574
00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:53.040
I suspect that those houses
will not last long,

575
00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:57.560
they probably have to build a
new one every year.

576
00:48:58.000 --> 00:48:59.840
Also termites eat them

577
00:48:59.960 --> 00:49:05.120
and tropical storms
that come through here tangle them.

578
00:49:05.640 --> 00:49:11.600
They have that kind of houses in the
neighbouring village that have fences

579
00:49:12.040 --> 00:49:19.000
made from the same material. It is
very nice to sleep in those houses.

580
00:49:19.400 --> 00:49:25.040
You can feel a quiet breeze blowing
through the house.

581
00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:27.840
There was a small storm and very
strong rain

582
00:49:27.960 --> 00:49:30.720
during the first night,
the roof leaked a bit.

583
00:49:31.240 --> 00:49:33.080
I was able to move my backpack

584
00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:36.360
from underneath the water coming in
at the right time.

585
00:49:36.480 --> 00:49:41.600
But yes, very enjoyable.
The floors are made of sand.

586
00:49:41.760 --> 00:49:46.840
Some cabins have a cement floor
but mine has a sand floor.

587
00:49:47.560 --> 00:49:52.280
I think that the village houses have
a sand floor as well.

588
00:49:55.040 --> 00:49:59.520
And they have this big sand bar here
before the ocean starts.

589
00:49:59.760 --> 00:50:03.440
I understand that it is a protection
for them

590
00:50:05.960 --> 00:50:10.480
so that when the sea levels are high
the water will not reach the village.

591
00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:31.080
There are endless numbers of people
in Africa who need help.

592
00:50:31.680 --> 00:50:36.840
It is another question how to help
them - give the poor people money or

593
00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:40.480
find opportunities how village people
could help themselves.

594
00:50:41.840 --> 00:50:43.640
By Diana's leading a choice
has been made

595
00:50:43.760 --> 00:50:45.520
for developing small business
enterprise.

596
00:50:46.280 --> 00:50:48.720
There is hope that through
that the basket weavers

597
00:50:48.840 --> 00:50:51.600
and Shea butter producers are able

598
00:50:51.760 --> 00:50:54.160
to manage independently without
help from Estonians.

599
00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:58.920
Currently there are 100 women
in Kongo village

600
00:50:59.120 --> 00:51:01.880
who are involved in producing Shea
butter but if there are more orders

601
00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:04.800
then 200 to 300 women are ready to
come along.

602
00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:07.720
Right now the first official order
has been placed,

603
00:51:08.920 --> 00:51:12.440
a two ton trial shipment goes
to the US market.

604
00:51:13.200 --> 00:51:15.680
Co-operative got the export
certificate and

605
00:51:16.200 --> 00:51:19.880
Shea butter quality test results
were very good.

606
00:51:21.800 --> 00:51:26.520
The basket weavers' co operative
started in autumn 2014 with 8 women.

607
00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:30.080
Today there are
15 active craftswomen.

608
00:51:31.160 --> 00:51:33.920
600 baskets that
have been sold in Estonia.

609
00:51:34.720 --> 00:51:37.440
The weavers have had help from
two Estonian designers.

610
00:51:39.760 --> 00:51:44.440
Mondo is helped by
Estonian volunteers,

611
00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:49.000
mainly experts, teachers
and medical workers

612
00:51:49.640 --> 00:51:51.920
Recently they were trying to find
someone to help explain

613
00:51:52.040 --> 00:51:54.480
the health risks involved in gold
mining.

614
00:51:55.720 --> 00:51:57.960
There is a plan to implement
the development and business model

615
00:51:58.080 --> 00:52:01.760
in the neighbouring villages that is
used and working in Kongo village.

616
00:52:02.120 --> 00:52:04.720
Locals value the work of Estonians
highly and express

617
00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:08.680
their great gratitude - it wouldn't
be surprising if one day

618
00:52:08.800 --> 00:52:12.760
Kongo village in Ghana was named
Estonian village.

619
00:52:21.000 --> 00:52:23.320
Diana's time has come to an end in
Ghana this time.

620
00:52:24.320 --> 00:52:26.160
Further work needs to be done
from Estonia

621
00:52:26.360 --> 00:52:30.000
but she will go back
to Africa in few months time.

622
00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:44.840
What brings me back to Africa?
I don't actually go to Africa,

623
00:52:45.200 --> 00:52:54.240
I go to Kongo village. If you have
created these social ties somewhere

624
00:52:56.520 --> 00:52:59.080
and started loving and respecting
these people

625
00:52:59.280 --> 00:53:04.800
then you want to come back here.
I deeply care for the people

626
00:53:06.560 --> 00:53:10.520
I work with and care for how they go.
I am grateful that

627
00:53:10.840 --> 00:53:14.400
I have not come here once
and then left.

628
00:53:14.800 --> 00:53:19.320
I have been able to observe during
four years how children grow up

629
00:53:20.000 --> 00:53:24.480
and go to school
and how people develop.
